  - cold start problem
  - reinforcement learning
  - Sheldrake
  - McDougall rat maze
abstract: >-
  A computational model of Rupert Sheldrake's morphic resonance
  hypothesis, implemented as a multi-agent reinforcement learning
  simulation of the McDougall rat maze experiment (1920-1954).
  Demonstrates that a performance-weighted shared Q-table (the
  "morphic field") transmits useful learning across agent
  generations with no genetic channel, and that fresh agents with
  zero ancestry immediately outperform the control baseline by 26%.
  Establishes the hypothesis as formally coherent, identifies the
  displaced control as the most powerful experimental design, and
  connects the morphic field architecture to practical AI design
  patterns for multi-agent knowledge transfer, cold-start
  mitigation, and stigmergic coordination.
